we kept using @Injectable() over classes, why? 
ans: Dependency Injection

example: 
UserModule.ts file 

@Module({
  providers: [UsersRepository]
})
export class UsersModule {}      //have UsersRepository stored in the providers 

1. Nest comes accross this code: 

@Injectable()
export class UsersService {
  constructor(
    private readonly usersRepository: UsersRepository
  ) {}
}

2. Nest Needs userRepository (instance of that class UsersRepository)
3. Nest sees UsersRepository inside the Providers in the module file [line 7]
4. Nest registers it in DI container
5. Nest created userRepository instance from the class userRepository
6. Nest Injects it inside the UsersService class where it is needed. [line 13]

no need to create the instances manually inside the classes by developers. 


